            <ArticleTitle>[Treatment of knee joint pain with superficial needling].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>261-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To compare therapeutic effects of superficial needling and body acupuncture on knee joint pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Ninety-six cases of knee joint pain were randomly divided into a treatment group of 48 cases treated with superficial needling, and a control group of 48 cases treated with body acupuncture.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The cured rate was 66.7% in the treatment group and 39.6% in the control group with a significant difference between the two groups (P&lt;0.01). The total effective rate was 100.0% in both the two groups. The needed therapeutic times of superficial needling was significantly less than that of the body acupuncture (P&lt; 0.05).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Superficial needling has a good therapeutic effect on knee joint pain with a shorter therapeutic course.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
